<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wartburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1067</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Wartburg Castle</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wartburg is a castle on a hill above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenach">Eisenach</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, traditionally founded around 1067 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-vii-duke-of-bavaria">Louis the Bearded</a>. It was the court of the Ludowingian landgraves, home of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-of-hungary">Saint Elizabeth of Hungary</a> and the setting of the legendary contest of minstrels. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> hid there in 1521 and 1522 and translated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> into German, and students held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wartburg-festival">Wartburg Festival</a> there in 1817. It is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div>
